= Bit | The basic unit of normal computers: a switch that is either 0 or 1.
= Phase | The "direction" part of an amplitude. You cannot see it in one measurement, but it decides how paths interfere.
= Hadamard gate (H) | A gate that turns |0⟩ into an equal mix of |0⟩ and |1⟩, and back again.
= CNOT | A two-qubit gate: it flips the second qubit only if the first qubit is 1. Used to make entanglement.
= Quantum algorithm | A step-by-step recipe for a quantum computer that uses superposition and interference.
= Grover's algorithm | A quantum search method that finds a marked item in about √N steps instead of about N.
= Shor's algorithm | A quantum method to factor big numbers fast by finding a repeating pattern (a period).
= No-cloning theorem | The rule that no machine can make a perfect copy of an unknown quantum state.
= Noise | Unwanted disturbances (heat, stray signals, imperfect controls) that cause errors in qubits.
= Quantum error correction | Spreading one qubit's information over many qubits so errors can be found and fixed.
= Superconducting qubit | A qubit made from a tiny superconducting circuit (often aluminium, niobium or tantalum on a silicon or sapphire chip), cooled to about 0.01 K.
= Josephson junction | Two superconductors separated by a very thin insulating layer. It is the key part of a superconducting qubit.
= Dilution refrigerator | A special fridge that cools chips to about 10 millikelvin, colder than outer space.
= Trapped-ion qubit | A qubit stored in a single charged atom held in place by electric fields in a vacuum and controlled with lasers.
= Neutral-atom qubit | A qubit stored in an uncharged atom held by a focused laser beam (an "optical tweezer").
= Spin qubit | A qubit stored in the spin of an electron, for example in a tiny silicon device similar to a transistor.
= Photonic qubit | A qubit carried by a particle of light (a photon), often guided through channels on a chip.
= NV centre | A defect in diamond (a nitrogen atom next to a gap) whose spin can act as a qubit, even at room temperature.
= Topological qubit | A proposed qubit that stores information in the overall pattern of a material, making it naturally resistant to small errors. Not yet clearly demonstrated.
= Majorana zero mode | A special state that may appear at the ends of certain superconducting nanowires. Pairs of them could form a topological qubit.
= Anyon | A kind of particle-like state that can exist only in 2D materials. Swapping (braiding) anyons can perform gates.
= Quantum advantage | When a quantum computer does a task faster or better than any practical normal computer.
= Amplitude | A complex number attached to each basis state; its squared size is a probability.
= Ancilla | An extra helper qubit used during a computation, e.g. to measure a syndrome.
= Bell state | One of four maximally entangled two-qubit states, e.g. (|00⟩+|11⟩)/√2.
= Bloch sphere | A sphere where each point on the surface is a single-qubit pure state.
= BQP | Problems quantum computers solve efficiently with bounded error.
= Clifford gates | Gates generated by H, S and CNOT; easy to simulate classically on their own.
= Coherence time | How long a qubit keeps its quantum behaviour (T1, T2).
= Decoherence | Loss of quantum behaviour due to the environment.
= Density matrix | A matrix ρ describing pure or mixed states.
= Entanglement | Correlation between qubits that can't be described by separate states for each.
= Fidelity | How close two states are, from 0 to 1.
= Gate | A unitary operation on qubits.
= Interference | Amplitudes adding up or cancelling out.
= Logical qubit | A protected qubit encoded in many physical qubits.
= Measurement | Reading a qubit; gives 0 or 1 randomly by the Born rule and collapses the state.
= NISQ | Noisy Intermediate-Scale Quantum: today's devices without full error correction.
= Oracle | A black-box function used inside an algorithm.
= Qubit | The basic unit of quantum information, α|0⟩ + β|1⟩.
= Shot | One run of a circuit ending in a measurement.
= Stabiliser | A Pauli operator that leaves the code states unchanged.
= Superposition | A combination of basis states with amplitudes.
= Syndrome | Measurement results that reveal which error occurred.
= Transpiler | Software that rewrites a circuit to fit a specific device.
= Unitary | A reversible matrix satisfying U†U = I.
= Ket | A column vector written |ψ⟩ that describes a quantum state.
= Bra | The conjugate-transpose row vector ⟨ψ| of a ket; it turns kets into numbers.
= Inner product | ⟨φ|ψ⟩, a single number measuring overlap; |⟨φ|ψ⟩|² is a probability.
= Outer product | |ψ⟩⟨φ|, a matrix built from a ket times a bra.
= Tensor product | The ⊗ operation that joins systems; |0⟩⊗|1⟩ = |01⟩, sizes multiply.
= Projector | A matrix P = |ψ⟩⟨ψ| with P² = P that keeps only the |ψ⟩ part of a state.
= Hamiltonian | The energy matrix of a system; it decides how states change in time.
= Expectation value | ⟨ψ|A|ψ⟩, the average result of measuring A many times.
